    {
      "text": "5.1 Interval Analysis",
      "bbox": [
        46.484989166259766,
        446.5278015136719,
        130.34298706054688,
        455.653564453125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "An interval in R is a closed and connected subset of R deﬁned as",
      "bbox": [
        46.484989166259766,
        470.4241027832031,
        305.504638671875,
        480.55609130859375
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "[x] = [x, x] = {x ∈R | x ≤x ≤x},",
      "bbox": [
        46.485015869140625,
        486.86590576171875,
        193.9730224609375,
        496.8797912597656
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "(5.1)",
      "bbox": [
        480.9427490234375,
        487.87213134765625,
        500.0274963378906,
        496.9978942871094
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "where x and x refer, respectively, to the minimal and maximal bounds of [x].",
      "bbox": [
        46.485748291015625,
        503.3179016113281,
        353.4117431640625,
        513.4501342773438
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "A box on the other side represents a vector [x] in Rn and is deﬁned as a Cartesian product of n intervals:",
      "bbox": [
        57.824371337890625,
        513.1530151367188,
        473.1538391113281,
        525.8970947265625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "[x] = [x1] × · · · × [xn].",
      "bbox": [
        46.4864501953125,
        532.2078857421875,
        142.40225219726562,
        543.3150634765625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "(5.2)",
      "bbox": [
        480.8965148925781,
        533.214111328125,
        500.03668212890625,
        542.33984375
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Henceforth, |[x]| will denote the length of the interval [x], IR will refer to the set of intervals in R, and IRn the set",
      "bbox": [
        46.486328125,
        546.048095703125,
        500.02587890625,
        558.7921142578125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "of boxes in Rn.",
      "bbox": [
        46.486480712890625,
        560.1664428710938,
        107.41264343261719,
        571.2391357421875
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Operations on Intervals and Boxes.",
      "bbox": [
        46.48537063598633,
        574.5693359375,
        187.73114013671875,
        583.5755615234375
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Firstly, set-theoretic operations (such as intersection and union) are applicable to intervals. Note that the inter-",
      "bbox": [
        57.82579803466797,
        587.025634765625,
        500.05572509765625,
        596.1513671875
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "section of two intervals is always an interval, whereas their union is not necessarily an interval. The interval union",
      "bbox": [
        46.48537063598633,
        599.472900390625,
        500.017822265625,
        608.5986328125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "of two intervals [x] and [y] is deﬁned by the following expression:",
      "bbox": [
        46.485382080078125,
        610.9228515625,
        311.7217102050781,
        621.0548095703125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "[x] ⊔[y] = [[x] ∪[y]] ,",
      "bbox": [
        46.48638153076172,
        627.3661499023438,
        141.93606567382812,
        637.5250244140625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "(5.3)",
      "bbox": [
        480.94342041015625,
        628.3723754882812,
        500.0281677246094,
        637.4981079101562
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "where interval union operation is denoted by ⊔whereas ∪refers to the set-theoretic union operation. The symbol",
      "bbox": [
        46.488372802734375,
        643.8094482421875,
        500.0472106933594,
        653.94140625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "[.] denotes the interval hull operator returning, for any set S in R, the smallest interval enclosing S [17].",
      "bbox": [
        46.48834228515625,
        656.265625,
        460.8448181152344,
        666.3975830078125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
